  "problem": {
    "id": "train-7016",
    "question": "At its current growth rate, a certain plant will be 80 feet tall after a year. If the plant grows at the same rate every month and is currently 20 feet tall, what's its monthly growth rate in feet?",
    "gold": "5",
    "answer": "If the plant is currently 20 feet tall, it will have grown by 80-20=<<80-20=60>>60 feet after a year.\nSince a year has 12 months, and the tree grows at the same rate every month, the tree grows at 60/12=<<60/12=5>>5 feet every month.\n#### 5"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the monthly growth rate of the plant, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the current height of the plant and the height it will be after one year.\n2. Calculate the total growth over the year.\n3. Determine the monthly growth rate by dividing the total growth by the number of months in a year.\n\nStep 1: Identify the current height and the height after one year.\nThe current height of the plant is 20 feet, and after one year, it will be 80 feet.\n\nStep 2: Calculate the total growth over the year.\nThe total growth is the difference between the height after one year and the current height.\n\\[ 80 \\text{ feet} - 20 \\text{ feet} = 60 \\text{ feet} \\]\n\nStep 3: Determine the monthly growth rate.\nThe plant grows 60 feet in one year, and there are 12 months in a year. Therefore, the monthly growth rate is the total growth divided by the number of months.\n\\[ \\text{Monthly growth rate} = \\frac{60 \\text{ feet}}{12 \\text{ months}} = 5 \\text{ feet/month} \\]\n\nThus, the monthly growth rate of the plant is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
